{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>הלוא.</b> And the meaning of שאת according to many interpreters is 'your sin is lifted,' but what seems correct to me is that שאת means 'face,' for it is written first 'and his face fell' [Genesis 4:6], and this is a sign of shame, like the meaning of 'how shall I lift my face' [Genesis 32:21], and its meaning is: if you do good, your face is lifted, and likewise 'for he lifts not his face from any blemish' (Job 11:15)." "<b>חטאת רובץ.</b> Some say 'sin' in place of 'iniquity,' and some interpreters say 'at the opening of the grave your iniquity will lie until the day of judgment,' and they interpret 'and its desire' as referring to Abel, and the meaning is: why are you angry that I accepted Abel's offering, when he is subject to your authority and you are like a ruler over him? And others said it refers to the [evil] inclination, but it is not written so. And these said that 'at the door of your house your sin lies' and it goes with you. And some say 'at the opening of the mouth,' like the meaning of 'guard the openings of your mouth' (Micah 7:5). And what seems correct to me is that חטאת is in construct state, and 'the inclination of man's heart lies with him.'" "<b>ואליך תשוקתו.</b> For the inclination will return to your obedience if you will it, and you have the power to rule over it."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {1 "Resolved the elliptic ואיננו כתוב ('it is not written') as referring to the word יצר being absent from the verse; translation preserves the compression."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:43:12.252742280Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}